      "kernel_source": "vetted golden (golden/kernels/apply_adam_f32.py, in-place 4-load/3-store)",
      "perf_miss_note": "Honest, documented perf miss. Memory-bound: a zero-arithmetic copy-only diagnostic of the identical 4-load/3-store f32 tensors floors at 16966-17647 ticks at every tile size (2048/4096/8192), ~2.1x the reference 8107. Reaching 0.70 needs gen <= 11581, which is below even the pure-memcpy floor -> infeasible by kernel tuning (camodel DMA-modeling wall). Double-buffering (unroll=2) overflows UB at TILE=2048 and yields no gain at TILE=1024; rsqrt/fused ops only shave the small compute term. Kept as a correctness-confirmed generative cell with the gap disclosed.",
      "comparability_note": "Not a like-for-like high-level comparison: the reference (ops-nn/optim/apply_adam/op_kernel/arch35/apply_adam_dag.h) hand-writes CalcMt/CalcVt/CalcVarT in register-fused SIMT micro-API (__VEC_SCOPE__, MicroAPI::RegTensor, MicroAPI::DataCopy, MaskReg), keeping the entire m_t/v_t/var_t chain in vector registers (4 copy-ins / 3 copy-outs, no UB round-trips for intermediates). pyasc2 emits only the high-level API and must materialize each intermediate tile in UB, which is exactly why it stays memory-bound. The miss is therefore SIMT-attributable, reinforcing the DMA-floor finding, not a pyasc codegen bug. See docs/perf-vs-ascendc-demo.md \"Reference programming model\".",
      "evidence_file": "evidence/perf-vs-ascendc/apply_adam-float32-20260810T170938.json",
      "source": "measured",
